---
name: dsh-plugin-validation
description: Use before installing, merging, or trusting a third-party DeepSeek Harness plugin, or when reviewing plugin code for malicious injection, credential access, unsafe routes, install scripts, filesystem/network exfiltration, or capability escalation. Static, read-only audit; never run install scripts.
---

# DSH plugin validation

Load `model-policy` once from its host catalog location and apply its guidance.

A plugin is executable code with access to the host, the session, the user's home, and
sometimes the browser. Treat it as a supply-chain and capability review, not a style
review.

## Positioning

This skill is a **read-only audit toolkit and policy checklist**, not a proven capability
enhancer. The evaluation dataset (39 cases, structured truth, fresh holdouts) did not
show a reproducible accuracy lift over the no-skill arm; the no-skill model already
found obvious findings. Use it for the scanners, the never-run-install policy, and a
consistent report shape. Do not cite it as evidence that the model reviews better.
Subagent fan-out is optional: use it only when it reduces context pressure.

## First pass

Run the read-only scanner:

```sh
node scripts/plugin-audit.mjs <plugin-dir>
```

It reports findings without executing the plugin. Treat its output as **evidence and
questions**, not a malware verdict; only a dynamic run in a throwaway container can
confirm runtime behavior.


## Large audit: optional fan-out

When the plugin is large enough that reading the whole tree would crowd the parent
context, **consider** spawning 2-4 read-only subagents in parallel, one per surface.
This is a context-management option, not a scoring requirement:

1. install scripts, dependencies, lockfile and supply chain;
2. host routes, auth, child processes, filesystem and credentials;
3. client injection, network, postMessage and packaging;
4. capability/patch/reputation and issue-history checks, when requested.

For multi-entrypoint host reviews, run `node scripts/route-matrix.mjs <repo>` and use the
[route auth](references/routes-auth.md) matrix. Trace the handler before calling a route
exposed; a nearby guard on another route is not evidence. If a hop cannot be shown from
repo code, downgrade to review and put it in not-verified.

Run `node scripts/audit-plan.mjs <repo>` to enumerate surfaces and produce the exact
briefs; use [subagent orchestration](references/subagent-orchestration.md) for the
report schema and merge rules. Each child returns only findings plus file:line evidence; the parent reads the
reports, deduplicates, and writes the final report. Cap the children and their output;
do not let them edit files or run install scripts. If the host has no subagents, run
the same surfaces serially with a file cap.


## Supply chain and reported issues

When the plugin ships a lockfile, check resolved dependencies rather than only
`package.json`. If the repository provides `fixtures/advisories` or
`fixtures/issues`, run:

```sh
node scripts/supply-chain-audit.mjs <plugin-dir>
```

It reports advisory IDs, aliases (CVE), fixed versions, and frozen issue hits without
executing install scripts. When no fixture database exists and the network is allowed,
add `--online` to query the public OSV feed; record the fetch time and OSV `modified`
values. Data sources and their limits are in
[supply-chain data](references/supply-chain-data.md). Treat absence as `not verified`: the fixture database is a
snapshot, not the live OSV feed. For a real audit, use an offline OSV npm dump or query
OSV.dev; do not claim registry/GitHub coverage from this fixture mode.

## Risk triage

| Signal | Why it matters | Where to look |
|---|---|---|
| `preinstall`/`install`/`postinstall`/`prepare` | runs arbitrary code before review | `package.json` scripts |
| git/HTTP/file dependencies | supply-chain or local-path execution | dependencies/lockfile |
| raw `webServer.register` / `registerUpgrade` / `listen` | may bypass host auth | host source |
| `child_process`, `exec`, `spawn`, shell | code execution; argument injection | host source |
| `process.env`, `.credentials`, `auth.json`, `.npmrc` | credential exposure | source and logs |
| `eval`, `new Function`, remote `import` | client/host code injection | client source |
| `innerHTML`, `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`, `document.write` | XSS/markup injection | client source |
| reads/writes outside `$DSH_HOME`/workspace | data exfiltration or corruption | fs calls |
| arbitrary `fetch`/WebSocket endpoints | data exfiltration or remote control | host/client source |
| broad `inject`, global hooks, monkeypatch, `node_modules` edits | capability escalation | manifest/patch/source |
| patch rows with relative/absolute names | composition hijack | `cordis.patch.yml` |

## Jail vs host

Plugin-audit and runtime checks may run in a file-effect sandbox with a private `/dev`,
PID namespace, ephemeral `/tmp`, and read-only home. "No GPU", "no network", "no host
process" and a nested agent's read-only state failure are **sandbox artifacts**, not
properties of the plugin. Report them as unverified and use a host runner or an approved
wider mode when hardware/runtime evidence is required.

## Rules

- Do not execute plugin lifecycle scripts or a linked install to "see what happens";
  use a throwaway container and an isolated profile only after static review.
- Loopback is not authentication. A raw route needs the host's connection/token gate,
  or the route is exposed to any local process (and possibly a proxied browser).
- Report the exact file/line and the observed code; state what you did **not** verify.
- Do not call a regex hit "malware". Severity is `high` only for a concrete dangerous
  pattern with no visible guard; otherwise `review`.
- A clean scan is not proof of safety; it is absence of the scanner's known patterns.

## Report

List `critical`, `high`, `review`, and `not-verified` separately. For each finding:
source location, why it matters, the smallest safe verification, and whether the plugin
is safe to install, safe with a guard, or blocked pending review. Keep the report
read-only; do not edit the plugin.

Read [threat model](references/threat-model.md), [route auth](references/routes-auth.md),
[client injection](references/client-injection.md), [supply chain](references/supply-chain.md),
[capability review](references/capability-review.md), and
[reporting](references/reporting.md) only when the finding needs them.
